====================================================================================================
 MarXus master equation
====================================================================================================
 RUN SETTINGS
 ------------
 Deck:               /home/peter/Dropbox/Research_Leuven/MarXus/validation/acetyl_o2_mesmer/input/acetyl_o2_250K.inp
 Method:             DIRECT TIME INTEGRATION of the grained populations, dN/dt = R F - J N, and of the yields of every exit
                     initial state: pulse N(0) = F (the normalized chemical-activation source)
                     output times 1e-12 .. 1e2 s, 4 per decade (operator of the final steady state)
 Solvers:            adaptive L-stable Rosenbrock method Rodas4 (adapted from KPP; Hairer, Wanner, Solving ODEs II, Sec. IV.7)
                     relative tolerance 1e-6, absolute 1e-14; steps of powers of two, banded Cholesky of s I + S reused
 Parallel:           4 cores (--ncore); the 1 conditions (T, p) in batches of 1 at a time (rayon)
                     BLAS threads per LAPACK call: 4
 Temperatures (K):   250
 Pressures (torr):   37.48
 Energy grid:        grains of 100.000 cm-1 on a common absolute scale, sums of 1 cm-1 counting cells
 Collisions:         exponential down, exactly normalized (Robertson (ed.), CCK 43 (2019), eq. 4.16), transitions up to 15 <dE_down>
                     low-energy reservoirs where eq. 4.16 fails (sparse states, Robertson 2019, p. 294): the grains from the
                       failing one down form one thermalized state (reservoir state, MESMER manual, Sec. 14.2.1):
                       T = 250 K: Int1 1 grains (100 cm-1, 56.4 k_BT below the lowest threshold)
                     collision frequency: Lennard-Jones (per well, below); collision integral Omega(2,2)*: Neufeld, Janzen, Aziz, J. Chem. Phys. 57, 1100 (1972) (CollisionIntegral in the deck)
 Tunneling:          exact Eckart transmission (Miller, J. Am. Chem. Soc. 101, 6810 (1979), eq. 8)
 Source:             thermal reactant R through B_R1 (R -> Int1)
                     F(E) ~ rho(E) k(E) exp(-E/kT) of the entrance channels (Pfeifle, Olzmann, IJCK 46, 231 (2014), eqs. 7, 9)
====================================================================================================
 CHEMICAL NETWORK
 ----------------

Wells (collisions: <dE_down>(T) = <dE_down>(T_ref) (T/T_ref)^n; Lennard-Jones collision frequency):
      Name   grains    absolute grains   collisions; sink
      Int1      171         -117 .. 53   <dE_down> = 130 cm-1 (T/300 K)^0; sigma 3.820 A, epsilon 69.47 K, reduced mass 3.797 amu
      Int2      160         -106 .. 53   <dE_down> = 130 cm-1 (T/300 K)^0; sigma 3.820 A, epsilon 69.47 K, reduced mass 3.797 amu

Channels (k(E) of every channel; rate model from the deck):
      Name     From       To   Model
      B_R1     Int1        R   barrierless, inverse Laplace transform; entrance of the reactant (source)
      B_R2     Int1     Int2   rigid transition state
      B_R4     Int1 P_ketene_HO_2   rigid transition state
      B_R2     Int2     Int1   rigid transition state
      B_R3     Int2 P_lactone_OH   rigid transition state

Energetics (kcal/mol, relative to R):

Wells (G - ground energy, D - lowest barrier out of the well):
      Name          G          D
      Int1     -33.34      -5.14
      Int2     -30.39      -8.49

Bimolecular (G - ground energy):
      Name          G
         R       0.00

Barriers (H - ZeroEnergy of the barrier in the deck):
      Name          H     From       To   Model
      B_R1       0.00     Int1        R   barrierless, inverse Laplace transform
      B_R2      -5.14     Int1     Int2   rigid transition state
      B_R3      -8.49     Int2 P_lactone_OH   rigid transition state
      B_R4       2.77     Int1 P_ketene_HO_2   rigid transition state

====================================================================================================
Units: unimolecular rate coefficients 1/s; bimolecular rate coefficients cm^3/s; yields in %.
Numbers with six significant digits; *** marks a value that is not available.
====================================================================================================

____________________________________________________________________________________________________
DIRECT TIME INTEGRATION OF THE MASTER EQUATION
____________________________________________________________________________________________________

dN/dt = R F - J N for the populations of all grains, and dY_x/dt = sum_E k_x(E) N(E) for the yield of every
exit (product channels, escape sinks), integrated with an adaptive, L-stable Rosenbrock method from the
initial state above: the early relaxation and the later chemistry in one integration, without a steady-state
assumption and without a separation of chemical and relaxation modes. For a pulse the total population +
yields stays 100%, and the yields at long times equal those of the final steady state (k_x^T J^-1 F).

